\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid15166504 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 Chapter 6 \endash  The Hunt is Joined
\par 
\par Redcoven was a bustling country city, nestled deep in the bread basket of Amuresca. Ebon Gables being near to Auldfuster, it was actually one of the only other cities I\rquote d visited more than once over the course of my life. Redcoven was hardly as lar
ge, densely-populated, or industrious as Auldfuster, but I found it a more pleasant place overall }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid6561256 because}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 
 of that.
\par Even so near to winter, the fields and farmland surrounding the city were a hive of activity, final harvests and shepherds with their flocks,
 roadside stands selling late-season vegetables and all manner of locally-made goods. I managed to pick up a few baked sweet potatoes being sold by a young cattle dog family who\rquote 
d set up a roasting fire at one of the crossroads miles outside of the city limits. It was all I could afford with the few copper coins I\rquote d managed to dig out from the pockets of my britches. And it was precisely what I\rquote 
d needed to lift my spirits, and put some strength back in my body.
\par Cattle dogs were particularly common here, it seemed. Hardly surprising, given the lake country was ideal grazing for goats and sheep.
 Seeing all the families traveling or selling on the roads here, as well as the cottages and steads nestled out in the rolling hills, it was easy to idealize this kind 
of place. I knew, of course, life out here was more complicated and difficult than it appeared\'85 the land only gave what it gave, and country lords were less wealthy and could be more prone to overburdening their vassals and overcharging as landlords
\'85 but it was pleasant at least to imagine a simpler life. If I hadn\rquote t been given up as a child, this is the sort of living I\rquote d have made.
\par I was grateful for the blessing of being raised by the Order, though. While perhaps simpler and more peaceful, a life as a tr
adesman or laborer would have meant little to no education, and I never would have known all that I was missing. The world was more open to me than it was to these people. And while a greater understanding 
came with the burden of knowing the depth of morta
l folly and suffering, it also meant I could appreciate the beauty of the changing seasons, and how they differed all over the world, from a learned perspective. Knowing how things were was one thing, knowing }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid7632043 why}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  was quite another. 
\par A commoner would not have access to the library I\rquote d grown up with, or the wisdom of monks on hand. I wouldn\rquote t have it any other way.
\par It was midday when I began growing close to the city, and the weather had finally cleared. The skies were spotless blue and the wind had let up. It was still cold, but an ideal late autumn day. I tried to ignore the fact that I couldn\rquote 
t breathe through my nose, and enjoyed the sights and scenery as I crossed those last few miles.
\par There was the occasional watch post quartered by the local militia, but overall, no major military presence in Redcoven. The road was primarily frequented by carriages and tradesmen, all regular, decent folk,
 so my armaments got more than a few looks. I kept my cloak up over my shoulder to make my vestments more apparent, (they were all I had to speak to my station any more) and did my best to set peoples\rquote 
 minds at ease. I hardly wanted to be mistaken for a mercenary of some sort.
\par I could see the steeple of the Church of Saint Rhine, easily twice the height of every other bui
lding in the city, elegant with its dark black filigree. The steeple was strung with crimson and gold flags, and the moons of the One True God perched at the very top, calling me home. I\rquote 
d spent months there in the dormitories as a youth, and explored the 
city with some of the other boys, including Nicholas. I needed no help finding my way through the city, even six years later. The Templar Ministry was a fortified building adjacent to the lot, understated in comparison to the grandeur that was Saint Rhine
\rquote s. 
\par I would go so far as to say Saint Rhine\rquote 
s was a cathedral, although they preferred humbler terminology. It was nearly five hundred years old and had long ago been a military outpost turned fort, with a church inside. It had been converted fully into a holy
 site after the last Huudari war, and had the distinction of housing an order of lay monks- rat kin- who had earned their place alongside the canines of the city in that conflict, standing arm-in-arm against the incursion of the lion tribes.
\par Being as they 
were rat-kin, the monks here could not ever serve or be ordained as full clergy, of course. But their order was unique, in that the local lords recognized them with nearly the same privileges and honor that would be associated with their canine brethren. 
It was my understanding that they\rquote d used their status in the city to lift the usual restrictions their people faced in settling in certain districts, and to afford them better options in the trades they were admitted to work in. 
\par What it amounted to was simple- there were nearly as many rats in Redcoven as canines, if not more. And the ones that lived here seemed more industrious, more civilized, better clothed and presentable. The only time in my life I\rquote 
d ever seen so many vermin-kind in one place was when we\rquote 
d briefly gone to the coast. They tended to live more in port cities, where they could easily find work in their accepted trades at the docks and on ships. Here, they seemed to do just about everything\'85 every manner of shop, craft, they even had their 
own public houses and buggy services for smaller peoples. 
\par I remembered it from my youth, but seeing it now as an adult, it was striking. As a child, I hadn\rquote t thought of how revolutionary a city like this was. And how }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid4589283 
comfortable}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  the general populace seemed with the species-mixing.  
\par I\rquote m certain there was tension, from time to time. But given how hollowed-out many of our other large cities were since the war, and the mass immigration of canines in particular to Carvecia over the last century\'85 why wasn\rquote t this
 being done, everywhere?
\par My thoughts were once again lost in one of those mind-tangling questions there were no easy answers to, when I should have been focusing on the task at hand. But I was weary and my mind wandered at the best of times. I found myself
 nearly passing the grounds surrounding Saint Rhine\rquote 
s, and pulled my mule to a stop beside one of the open iron gates, my eyes drawn to the yard. I was coming in behind the Rectory, the narrow, cobbled street here squeezed in against the back of several sh
ops in the market district. It was an entrance not terribly well-known to the public, but how we\rquote d always come in and out as children. The back yard was for ceremonies and morning exertions, and it seemed the latter was taking place now
. Although given it was the afternoon, the boys running laps around the yard must have been getting their exercise in before lunch.
\par My stomach growled, and I knocked it with my fist, irritated. \'93Don\rquote t you complain, now,\'94 I murmured. The potatoes had been a few hours ago, and apparently, they hadn\rquote t been enough to make up for the last few days.
\par \'93Sir?\'94 a thready voice called out across the small field. I looked up in time to see one of the young lads- barely older than I\rquote d been when I\rquote d come here- coming to a jogging stop mid-st
ride. Two others took the opportunity to do the same, hands on their knees behind him, tongues lolling out panting despite the cold air. They\rquote d been at it a while by the look of them.
\par The young canines all inevitably turned to regard me curiously, the first who\rquote d called out to me hesitantly crossing the field towards the gate, while asking loudly, \'93
Are you here from the Ministry? Father Whitfield was given to expect someone this week, I think. That you?\'94
\par I dismounted, stepped through the open gates and tugged off my riding gloves, flexing my stiff, cold fingers. As I approached them, one of the other boys beside the first jabbed him with an elbow in the ribs
, and they muttered something to one another quietly.
\par When I made it up to them, the lad\rquote s ears were down and he looked cowed. He straightened his posture and mumbled, \'93M\rquote sorry for speaking informally, Brother Templar. Won\rquote t happen again.\'94
\par I huffed out a breathless laugh. I\rquote d crossed the threshold, had I? A year ago I\rquote d have considered myself amongst these boys, a young Brother-in-training. Now they were being deferential to me. I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid6633942 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  been ordained, I supposed. It felt strange\'85 most of the other young Brothers and Sisters I knew, I\rquote 
d grown up with. No such formality there.
\par \'93Set yourself at ease, young Brothers,\'94 I assured them. \'93And no, I ah\'85 I doubt I\rquote m the man from the Ministry your Father is expecting. I\rquote m just passing through the area.\'94
\par \'93Oh,\'94 the lad\rquote s ears perked up, \'93well, then-\'93
\par \'93I don\rquote t hear paws on dirt!\'94 an older man\rquote s voice called out from the Rectory doorway. Every single boys\rquote  fur suddenly stood on end, and I knew that look. I got it every time Father Helstrom had yelled at me growing up.
\par The old Father who came trudging across the green was a retriever with more grey than gold on his muzzle, and one bad eye. He walked with a slight hobble, but not so severe as to need a cane, like my Father did. 
\par He stopped when he saw me, and fastidiously tugged at the sleeves of his robe to pull them back down over his wrists from where he\rquote d had them gathered up. Penning something, most likely.
\par \'93You must be the young lad the Ministry said we should expect,\'94 he said, hurrying a bit more as he crossed the yard towards us.
\par \'93I don\rquote t think so Father,\'94 I shook my head, \'93I-\'93
\par \'93Yes, yes!\'94 he insisted. \'93You\rquote re the young Templar from Ebon Gables pursuing the Heart Thief investigation. You must be.\'94 He surely noticed the surprised expression on my face, because he waved an ink-stained hand. \'93
We received word weeks ago that you\rquote d be in the area looking into this case. A Father\'85 Helstrom\'85 informed us you\rquote 
d spent time in our care in the past, and would be likely to take refuge here if you came through the city. You are welcome, Brother!\'94
\par Stunned, I opened my mouth to thank the man, but the deluge of good news continued.
\par \'93The Ministry sent home one of our local Sons, actually,\'94 he said, \'93
just last week, on orders that I believe may coincide with your mission. It was all so sudden- he was on Inquisition with the Eastern Sect for the last three years, with the Border Lords. Nasty work\'85 we\rquote 
re very glad to have him home, and if he can assist you with this investigation of yours, I believe he will be eager to do so.\'94
\par \'93An Inquisitor?\'94 My heart leapt into my throat. \'93The Ministry sent a seasoned Inquisitor?\'94
\par The Father only smiled at me, perhaps picking up on the excitement in my voice. \'93Yes, young lad. They seem to think you\rquote re on to something.\'94
\par 
\par 
\par I checked the address I\rquote d been given again, to be certain I\rquote d not be surprising a common family in their home. But everything about the building was as described by Father Whitfield, the retriever from Saint Rhine\rquote s. 
\par Relatively new construction, dark brick, the apartment itself settled in over a clerk\rquote s office. I was glad I\rquote d been able to leave the mule at the Church, because there was very little room on the street for her
. All Inquisitors were afforded a reliable horse and tack for their work, but I assumed the man probably kept his at the Church, in their spacious stables. This residence wasn\rquote t far.
\par I began to ascend the narrow external staircase, when the door to the curtained Clerk\rquote s office opened and a large man stepped out. Or rather, leaned partially out, far enough that he could lock eyes with me and look me over.
\par He was canine, a dog for certain, but vaguely wolfish in features like many of the wilder breeds tended to be. He had a thick, long coat of white and light fawn, with a dark brown and black mask around his eyes and down his neck. He looked older than me,
 but still far younger than most of my other Seniors, and had clearly led a dangerous life, judging by the scars crisscrossing his muzzle.
\par And he possessed two of the most piercing aquamarine eyes I had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid14767982 ever}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 
 seen. Sharp as a blade, and just as capable of cutting through you, I\rquote d imagine. I felt like the man could }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid14767982 see}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  my sins upon my flesh, as clear as day. I knew that look. It was the same calculating, knowing stare my Father would level at me, when I deserved it.
\par I turned on the stoop, fixing my arms at my sides, properly. \'93Inquisitor,\'94 I said, guessing but very certain of my assumption.
\par The man gave no initial response, save to look me up and down once more, then step aside the doorway, gesturing for me to come down.
\par I made my way to him, and hesitated only a moment once I was in the threshold of the building, because the fellow\rquote s sheer }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid14767982 presence}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  nearly knocked me back. He was large and imposing for one, taller than me, his ears nearly touching the top of the door frame. But it was more than just a physical presence\'85 the air around him felt }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid1320560 heavier}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 , somehow. It was an aura that all but demanded respect, and compliance. 
\par He wasn\rquote t even wearing his vestments, or armor of any kind. Although I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid1320560 did}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 
 note the blade at his hip, with one of his thick palms resting casually above it on the pommel. He watched me the whole way to him, and his eyes continued to follow me as I entered the \lquote office\rquote 
. There was nothing intentionally intimidating about his affect, but then there didn\rquote t need to be. His expression was, if anything, calm. Composed. No outward signs of bristling, teeth or flexing claws\'85 but humorless. 
\par The door closed behind me.
\par I knew immediately this was a Ministry Archive, despite having never seen one before. That explained why he was living above it. The walls were nothing but lockboxes and rows of shelves, stacked with doc
uments and ledgers, containing the secrets of our Kingdom. I tried not to stare like a fool at the trove\'85 only marginally succeeding.
\par \'93I shouldn\rquote t be permitted in here yet,\'94 I uttered quietly, before I could consider my words might cause offense.
\par \'93You are Isidor of Ebon Gables,\'94 he spoke from behind me, and strode up to my side, remarkably light-footed for a man his size. 
\par It hadn\rquote t been a question.
\par \'93Yes, Inquisitor,\'94 I replied, my body working on muscle memory in my responses. I\rquote d }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid10239197 never}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 
 been in the presence of someone who\'85 he must have been a well-regarded, senior member of the Order, if they\rquote d entrusted him with an Archive. Even Father Helstrom had to be granted permission to have access to a place like this, since he\rquote 
d retired. 
\par He stepped around to my front, regarding me from barely a foot away. He looked at me for a time, before tilting his head just-so. \'93You don\rquote t remember me,\'94 he said at length. Again, not a question.
\par \'93Have we met, Inquisitor?\'94 I asked, searching my memory.
\par The first hint of something approaching an expression crossed his features. Maybe fondness, but if so, muted. \'93You were barely up to my chest, then. And it was the summer, I believe, so\'85
 my fur would have been trimmed back. It grows into quite the mane in the winter months.\'94
\par Oh, Lord. There it was. A flash of memory-
\par \'93Small arms training,\'94 I said in realization. \'93You\'85 Brother Malachi?\'94
\par Finally, the quirk of something that was most certainly a smile. Diluted by his overall seriousness, but still unmistakably there. \'93You do remember.\'94
\par \'93I remember,\'94 I let out a breath, sensing the air lightening in the room, \'93being knocked over into the dirt like I\rquote d run into a brick wall, every time I got cocky and you shield-checked me.\'94
\par His chest rumbled in a baritone laugh that he didn\rquote t bother opening his muzzle for. \'93You were small for your age, then. I recall your Brother monk being concerned you would be atrophied due to some\'85 affliction\'85
 and never fill out. I am very glad to see he was wrong.\'94 His eyes moved over me again. \'93You\rquote ve become quite the strapping young fellow, Brother Isidor. How is your form these days?\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m certain you would still be able to put me in the dirt,\'94 I assured him with a quirk of a smile, \'93Brother Malachi. But\'85 better, I hope. Far better. I have taken my training very seriously.\'94
\par \'93I can tell,\'94 he assured me, gesturing towards the one wooden table and chairs in the corner of the room, stacked in papers and ledgers, as well as what looked like a local map. \'93And not just because you look well and strong. I\rquote 
ve been studying your work, since I was recalled to Redcoven. The Ministry is building a file based on }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid1325172 your}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  
investigation.\'94
\par My chest felt fit to burst. I could hardly get the words out, the relief that was suffusing me was so profound, I genuinely felt I might cry if I were not careful. I swallowed back stone after stone in my throat, or at least that\rquote s how it felt, 
and finally managed to breathlessly say, \'93I\rquote m so glad. I was worried\'85 well, that no one was listening.\'94
\par \'93I am very sorry,\'94 he said, glancing back once over his shoulder as he stepped up to the table, picking up a few letters with broken wax seals on them. \'93I\rquote 
m certain it must have been trying, going at this alone. But I assure you, the Ministry has been reviewing everything you\rquote ve sent. It\rquote s just taken time to find its way to the proper channels. And now that it has, we are taking this all very,
\'94 he looked back up from the letters to me, \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid15760206 very}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  seriously.\'94
\par \'93Truly?\'94 
\par \'93If they were willing to dispatch me from my appointment with the Border Lords,\'94 he said, \'93then you may be certain they consider this important.\'94
\par \'93She is here in Redcoven,\'94 I said quickly. \'93Or was, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid10816216 very}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  recently. We should begin canvasing-\'93

\par \'93Hold,\'94 he put up a hand, his masked brow narrowing. \'93You think the Heart Thief is in }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid10816216 my}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 
 city? Now?\'94
\par \'93I am certain of it,\'94 I said, reaching quickly beneath my cloak to my main belt pouch, where I had the payment note tucked away. I produced it and handed it to him still folded.
\par He opened it carefully and scanned it quickly, not bothering to waste time and asking while he read, \'93Significance? What am I looking for?\'94
\par \'93I have followed sightings, too particular to be any other feline,\'94 I said, \'93and I must impress upon you- she does change her appearance, but I have met her face-to-face, so I am very sure-\'93
\par He cut me off. \'93You need not argue your case there, Brother Isidor. You have found more information on this scum in the last two months than the entirety of the Inquisition has in two years. You\rquote 
ve even obtained physical evidence we can use to convict her, and physical descriptors of one of her disguises that has doubtless thwarted our previous attempts to identify her. All without access to Archives, or backing of any kind. I have }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid8744884 no}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  doubt that you can find this woman. I\rquote m here to afford you the support you deserve, and take point 
in the matters a junior Templar cannot.\'94
\par He reached out and settled a heavy hand on my shoulder, squeezing hard enough that I felt it even through my weathered, travel-worn, damp clothing and cloak. I let out a shuddering breath I hadn\rquote 
t known I was holding, my whole body going slack. And God bless him, he didn\rquote t tell me to correct my posture.
\par In fact when I looked up, he was leveling that intense gaze at me. But it didn\rquote t feel invasive. 
\par He was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid8744884 listening}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  to me.
\par \'93Please continue,\'94 he pressed.
\par \'93I had\'85\'94 I paused, \'93\'85 a near-sighting, two towns to the west. It\rquote s a bit of a long story, but\'85\'94
\par \'93A \lquote near-sighting\rquote ?\'94 He quirked his head, then huffed. \'93Sounds interesting. Alright. I want to hear all of it. Every detail you can possibly relate. But perhaps you should sit. You\rquote 
ve been on the chase for some time now, yes? I know how that is. I mean no offense, but you appear run-down.\'94
\par My stomach chose that exact moment to announce its hunger.
\par We both looked down. His paw shifted off my shoulder and bumped my chin up to face him. \'93When is the last time you ate?\'94 he demanded.
\par \'93I had some potatoes earlier today,\'94 I insisted. \'93I\rquote ve been making do on low rations since my coin\'85 well\'85\'94
\par \'93Mnh,\'94 he grunted, tugging out a chair for me. \'93You need meat. Will mutton do? I\rquote ve some in the back. Bread, too. Cheese?\'94
\par \'93I,\'94 I stammered. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid8744884 God}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 , that all sounded incredible. \'93I probably should eat something,\'94
 I agreed. \'93But I won\rquote t abuse your hospitality, a little will do.\'94 I sighed. \'93Maybe I\rquote ll actually make my next weigh-in.\'94
\par \'93Lord, they still have you doing those?\'94 I heard him grumble, almost beneath his breath. \'93Those will stop once you\rquote re accepted by the Ministry. They aren\rquote t designed for big fellows like us, anyway. Come on. You can eat and talk.\'94

\par I followed him, dumbstruck and so overwhelmed with gratitude from what the day had brought me, I could barely find the words. 
\par We did speak over a meal, which he partook in as well. I told him everything\'85
 or, near enough. Certain personal details were better left for Confession with a Priest, but I withheld nothing about the case. He listened intently, only occasionally interjectin
g to ask questions, with a seriousness and fixation that I knew meant he was truly hearing me. He wrote down nearly everything I told him, making personal notes for himself in the margins. 
\par It went on like that for nearly an hour. 
\par I was dizzy by the end of it, relieved down to my bones. So much so that I felt liable to drift off, my body choosing to remind me in my state of rest, the warmth of the hearth uncoiling my muscles, and a full stomach, that I hadn\rquote 
t really gotten a complete night\rquote s sleep in\'85 days. 
\par And then we got to the part about the payment note, and he stood, suddenly.
\par The motion jarred me back, and I watched him as he strode to the corner of the room, opening one of the lockboxes and sorting through the ledgers there.
\par \'93Brother Malachi? What is it?\'94 I asked, watching him.
\par \'93Rathborne,\'94 he said, distractedly. \'93I know the name.\'94
\par \'93As do I,\'94 I said. \'93Although I\rquote m not certain why, or how. Some kind of incident\'85 some kind of scandal with the family, maybe?\'94
\par \'93A mistress, if memory serves,\'94 he said, fingering through the ledgers until one caught his eyes. I stood and moved to join him, looking past his shoulder as he opened the file, scanning through it quickly. \'93I remember, because,\'94 he 
skimmed a finger pad across the paper, pausing on one spot and tapping it with a claw, \'93there is a branch family here in Redcoven. Distant relatives of the late Lord Rathborne. Yes, here.\'94
\par He held the written account up for me to see. \'93The investigation was ordered by the Wellford line, extremely well-connected, wealthy family out of Amurfolk. Their daughter was arranged to marry the only surviving son, the inheritor.\'94
 He spoke quickly as he read through, \'93They were concerned about him\'85 mhm\'85 rumors of impropriety with a servant, or more than one. E
ven after his move to Amurfolk, you see here? There was talk there was a mistress. The Wellfords took it seriously enough to contact the Ministry.\'94
\par \'93God, there he is,\'94 I said, gasping quietly, the name standing out to me all over the paper. \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid2518671 He\rquote s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  the young lord in question.\'94
\par \'93Cillian Rathborne,\'94 he said, the man\rquote s name uttered like a pronouncement in the quiet room. \'93They never found the mistress. The marriage went through. But the Inquisitor made multiple mentions here\'85 of a feline woman. A ghost they coul
d never locate.\'94
\par \'93He\rquote s still carrying on with her,\'94 I whispered, the words sticking in my throat like thorns. Why did the realization shock me so? Why did it make it so hard to breathe, all of a sudden?
\par \'93We long suspected she had Pedigree backing,\'94 he murmured. \'93A benefactor\'85 a paramour\'85 someone feeding her information and hiding her. Here. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid11097659 Here}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  he bloody is.\'94
\par He turned his head towards me, the hint of a fang in his subtle grin, his gaze hungry like a dyre. And I suddenly realized what it was I\rquote d been feeling, back in the doorway.
\par This is how prey felt. Being hunted.
\par This is probably how she felt being hunted by me.
\par \'93Less than one hour with access to the Archives, and just the two of us talking,\'94 he said, \'93and we\rquote ve }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid11097659 got}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  something. Something we can use not just to hang }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid11097659 her}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504 , but the bastard who
\rquote s been aiding her. You\rquote re a savant, Brother. You and I are going to bring this little fiend down, and then I\rquote m going to tear the truth out of her. Bone\'85 by bone, if I must.\'94
\par I looked between him and the paper, in mute shock. My stomach lurched, threatening to upend the meal I\rquote d just had. I wasn\rquote t sure }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid15166504\charrsid11097659 what}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid15166504  I was feeling, it was all so disjointed and overwhelmed.
\par His body stiffened beside mine, suddenly. Before I could ask why, he had shoved the lockbox closed and was crossing the room, going for the peg rack his gear was hung on, beside my own. I\rquote d only just dressed down before the meal.
\par \'93Inquisitor?\'94 I asked, my voice wobbly. \'93What-\'93
\par \'93Harness up,\'94 he said, his authoritative tone brooking no argument. \'93The Rathborne family has a house in the city.\'94
\par \'93What?\'94 I blinked. Shouldn\rquote t that have been the first thing he\rquote d told me?
\par \'93The branch family,\'94 he explained quickly, shouldering into his own harness at a speed and efficiency that put my own to shame. \'93Go by the name \lquote Lane\rquote . They have a city home, here. They don\rquote t spend the winters here. It
\rquote s just servant staff and the elderly couple there now. It\rquote s practically an empty house.\'94
\par \'93Shit,\'94 I said hurriedly, falling back on profanity I should have been ashamed of, but I earnestly could not think what else to say. I understood the rush he was in quite suddenly, and moved quickly to gear up beside him.
